        "# Results {#sec-results}\n",
        "\n",
        "## Structure, contents, and where the data are held {#sec-structure}\n",
        "\n",
        "The resource is three comma-separated files, deposited on Zenodo under a DOI that will be minted at acceptance <!-- OUTLINE run-list item 10 --> and described in @tbl-release: a simulated registry file of `{python} registry_simulants` simulated people and two finder files of `{python} finder_500k_simulants` and `{python} finder_100k_simulants` simulated people respectively. All three are drawn from Florida residents in the simulated 2020, 2030 or 2040 census, a pool of `{python} fl_pool` distinct simulants. \n",
        "\n",
        "Every row carries a `simulant_id`, the identifier of the simulated person it describes, and the same identifier means the same person in all three files; this is the ground truth against which any linkage of the files can be scored. The remaining fields are the ones a linkage runs on: first name, middle initial, last name, date of birth, sex, race/ethnicity, street number, street name, unit number, city, state, ZIP code and SSN. Registry rows also carry a diagnosis date, which falls in 2020, 2030 or 2040. Addresses are Florida addresses down to city and ZIP code, with simulated rather than real streets. The registry has `{python} registry_rows` rows for its `{python} registry_simulants` people, and the finder files have `{python} n(files[\"finder_500k.csv\"][\"rows\"])` and `{python} n(files[\"finder_100k.csv\"][\"rows\"])` rows for `{python} finder_500k_simulants` and `{python} finder_100k_simulants` people; the few people with two rows are duplicated records (@sec-methods).\n",
        "\n",
        "The larger finder file shares `{python} overlap_500k_simulants` people with the registry, `{python} pct(match_prevalence_500k, 0)` of its records, and the smaller shares `{python} overlap_100k_simulants`, the same share; the two finder files share no people with each other. Because the key is exact, every finder record is either a true match to one registry person or a true non-match, and the `{python} overlap_500k_pairs` and `{python} overlap_100k_pairs` true record pairs are known rather than estimated. Alongside the files, the deposit carries a manifest of MD5 and SHA-256 digests (@tbl-release gives the MD5) and the configuration file that names every seed and design target, and the pipeline that built them is archived under its own DOI <!-- OUTLINE run-list item 10: code DOI -->. A second build from that configuration reproduced all three files byte for byte."
      ],
      "id": "bd32b814"
